3) What is REST API and its Architecture ?
      REST API, stands for Representational State Transfer Application Programming Interface
      - REST is stateless ( THe server doesnt remember previous requests from the client. Every request must contain all
        the info needed to process it.)

   Principles of REST API
   ----------------------
      - Client-server Architecture
      - Stateless
      - Can be cached
      - Opacque in terms of layers
      - Uniform interface (all the end points that are designed are uniform in nature)

   Web Services built following the REST architectural style are known as RESTFUL web services

   Benefits
   --------
      - Simplicity ( working on http protocals makes REST API simple to use and implement )
      - Scalability ( since they are stateless in nature means they can scale large number of requests )
      - Flexibility ( Data is not tied to resources or methods which allows for more flexibility in how data is returned )
      - Visibility ( Communication btw client and server is done using standard http methods )


4) http VS https
      1) Both of these are protocals that are used for transmitting infomation over the internet ( across the world wide web) .
      2) They operate based on a client-server model, where a client( web browser ) sends a req to the server hosting a website.
      3) Both protocols use similar methods to perform actions on the web server as well as status codes
      4) HTTP and HTTPS are both stateless protocols, meaning they dont inherently remember anything about the previous web session
      5) Both HTTP and HTTPs can transfer data in various formats including HTML, XML, JSON and plain text

    -  http stands for Hper Text Transfer Protocol
    -  https stands for Hper Text Transfer Protocal Secure  
      
      HTTPS is essentially HTTP with security ( The info that is transmitted using https is more secure and integrity of 
      the info is well mainrained in HTTPS )

