14) Understanding Components and ComponentScan
      Component
      ==========
            Component refers to a java class that is managed by the SPring IOC container.
            @Component is an annotation used to tell Spring that a class is a Spring Bean and should be managed by Spring IOC Container.
            @Component is used to mark a class as a Spring-managed bean so that Spring can automatically create and manage its object.
            Defining Components in Spring :
            ------------------------------
                  - USing XML
                  - Using Annotations
      
                  Using XML
                  ---------
                        <bean id="myCompoent" class="com.example.MyComponent"/>
       
                  Using Annotation
                  ----------------
                        import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
      
                        @Component //Marks the class a Spring Component
                        public class MyComponent {
                              //Class implementation
                        }
      
      Component Scanning
      ==================
            - Component Scanning is a feature helps to automatically detect and register beans from predefined package paths.
            - @componentScan is used to scan packages for Spring components and register them as beans in the Spring container.
            - @ComponentScan is used to tell sPring where to search for classes annotated with:
                  * @Component
                  * @Service
                  * @Repository
                  * @Controller
             After finding them, spring automatically creates beans and stores them in the IOC container.


15) @Value Annotation
      - @Value is used to inject values from properties files, environment variables, or hardcoded values into a Spring bean.
      - @Value is used to read and inject values from application.properties or application.yml into java variables.

      Ex:
            application.properties
                  app.name=Student Management System
            Java class
                  @Component
                  public class AppInfo {
                  
                      @Value("${app.name}")
                      private String appName;
                  
                      public void display() {
                          System.out.println(appName);
                      }
                  }
            Output :
                  StudentManagement System

            Hardcoded Value
            ---------------
                  @Value("Hello Spring Boot")
                  private String message;

            Default Value
            ------------
                  @Value("${server.port:8080}")
                  private int port;
      

16) @Autowired Annotation
      - @Autowired is used for Dependency Injection (DI). It tells Spring automatically provide the required bean object.
      - @Autowired is used to automatically inject one Spring bean into another bean.

17) @Qualifier Annotation
      - @Qualifier is used along with @Autowired to specify which bean should be injected when multiple beans of the same type are available.
      - @Qualifier is used to resolve the ambiguity when more than one bean of the same type exists in the Spring container.

18) What is Spring Boot?
      - Open-source, java-based framework used to create stand-alone production-guide Spring-based Application.
      - Spring Boot is an extension of the Spring framework that simplifies Java application development. It provides auto-configuration, starter dependencies,
         and embedded servers, which help developers build production-ready applications quickly with minimal configuration.

            Spring Boot = Spring Framework + prebuilt Configuration + Embedded Servers


19) Features of Spring Boot?
       Spring Boot provides several features such as :
            - Starter Dependencies
            - Auto Configuration
            - Embdedded Server (Tomcat)
            - Spring Boot Actuator
            - Spring Boot DevTools
            - Microservices Support
            - Less Configuration

            1) Starter Dependencies
               ----------------------
                  - Starter Dependencies are pre-packed Maven/Gradle dependencies that contain all required libraries for a specific feature.
                  - Starter dependencies provide a convenient way to include all required libraries for a specific functionality without manually adding each dependency.

            2) Auto Configuration
               -------------------
                  - Spring Booot automatically configures beans based on the dependencies available in the class path.

            3) Embedded Server
               ---------------
                  - Spring Boot comes with an embedded Tomcat, Jetty or Undertow server.
                  - Embedded server allows spring Boot applications to run independently without deploying them to an external server.

            4) Actuator
               --------
                  - Provides production-ready monitoring features.
                  - Spring Boot Actuator helps monitor and manage applications through health checks, metrics and other production endpoints.

            5) DevTools
               --------
                  - Improves developer productivity
                  - Spring Boot DevTools automatically restarts the application when code changes are made, improving development efficiency.

            6) Microservices Support
               ----------------------
                  - Spring Boot is widely used for building microservices
                  - Spring Boot provides excellent support for developing lightweight and scalable microservices,


20) @GetMapping
      - @GetMapping is a Spring Boot annotation used to handle HTTP GET requests. It is mainly used to retrieve data from server.
      - @GetMapping is an annotation in Spring Boot that maps HTTP GET requests to a controller method. It is used to fetch or read data from the server.


21) application.properties
      - application.properties is a central configuration file in spring boot used to define application settings without changing java code.
      - application.properties is a configuration file in spring boot used to store application settings such as server port, database connection details,
         application name, logging configuration and other properties.

22) @RestController
      @RestController is a Spring Boot annotation used to create RESTful web services. It is a combination of
        @Controller and @ResponseBody, so the return value of methods is sent directly as the HTTP response, usually in JSON format.
      
23) @PostMapping
      @PostMapping is a Spring Boot annotation used to map HTTP POST requests to a controller method. It is commonly used to create or save data received from 
       the client. It is a short cut for @RequestMapping(method = RequestMEthod.POST)

24) @RequestBody
      @RequestBody is a Spring Boot annotation used to bind the HTTP request body(usually JSON) to a Java object. Spring automatically converts the JSON data into the
       corresponding Java object.

25) Postman
      Postman is an API testing tool used to send HTTP requests such as GET, POST, PUT and DELETE and verify the responses received from the server.
       It helps developers test and debug REST APIs.


26) How do you get JSON from a SPring Boot application?
      1. Create an Entity/POJO class
      2. Create an object of that class
      3. Return the object from a method inside @RestController
      4. Spring Boot uses Jackson to convert the Java object into JSON.
      5. Call the API using Postman.
      6. The JSON response is displayed in Postman.

27) @PathVaribale
      @PathVariable is used to get a value from the URL path and pass it to a method parameter. It is commonly used to fetch a specific record by ID.
            Ex:
                  @GetMapping("/students/{id}")
                  public String getStudent(@PathVariable int id) {
                      return "Student Id: " + id;
                  }
            URL: 
                  http://localhost:8080/students/101
            Output:
                  Student Id: 101

28) Controller Layer (@RestController)
